WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:01.620
Instructor: Alright, so in this video

00:01.620 --> 00:02.453
I'm gonna run through

00:02.453 --> 00:05.430
some of the essential terminal commands for navigation,

00:05.430 --> 00:07.920
for moving around on your machine.

00:07.920 --> 00:09.990
We need this in order to work with Git.

00:09.990 --> 00:11.790
If you already know these commands,

00:11.790 --> 00:16.200
things like ls and pwd and cd,

00:16.200 --> 00:18.960
if you know those commands, feel free to skip this of course

00:18.960 --> 00:21.750
but if you don't, I'm gonna run through this quickly.

00:21.750 --> 00:24.600
But also I recommend you take some time to practice

00:24.600 --> 00:27.030
and just keep a reference or a cheat sheet up

00:27.030 --> 00:28.320
that you can look back at

00:28.320 --> 00:30.030
because I'll be using a lot of these commands

00:30.030 --> 00:30.930
throughout the course.

00:30.930 --> 00:33.000
But nothing super difficult, I promise,

00:33.000 --> 00:35.040
the hardest part is just remembering them.

00:35.040 --> 00:37.800
Okay, so I've got my Mac window here,

00:37.800 --> 00:40.650
I've got my terminal open, just the terminal app on Mac

00:40.650 --> 00:42.387
so if you're following along with a Mac,

00:42.387 --> 00:43.860
you'll look at this screen.

00:43.860 --> 00:46.800
And then occasionally when there is a small deviation

00:46.800 --> 00:48.444
or difference on a Windows machine,

00:48.444 --> 00:52.080
here is my Windows terminal, this is the Bash

00:52.080 --> 00:54.240
or Git Bash shell that I've opened up

00:54.240 --> 00:56.460
so if you installed that on a Windows machine,

00:56.460 --> 00:58.140
it will look something like this.

00:58.140 --> 01:00.750
So the first command we'll talk about is called ls

01:00.750 --> 01:02.460
it is short for list.

01:02.460 --> 01:06.990
List, the contents of a folder or of the current directory.

01:06.990 --> 01:10.290
So if you type ls into your terminal and you hit Enter,

01:10.290 --> 01:12.240
wherever you currently are,

01:12.240 --> 01:13.980
and we haven't learned how to move yet

01:13.980 --> 01:15.780
so we'll talk about where we currently are,

01:15.780 --> 01:17.280
wherever that location is,

01:17.280 --> 01:19.770
we will see the contents printed out.

01:19.770 --> 01:21.180
So in this case, I'm in a folder

01:21.180 --> 01:23.160
that contains some JavaScript files

01:23.160 --> 01:27.030
a HTML file, a CSS file, a llamas Python file,

01:27.030 --> 01:28.530
a folder called Secret,

01:28.530 --> 01:31.560
that is what's printed out at least in this slide.

01:31.560 --> 01:33.210
So if I try it over here,

01:33.210 --> 01:34.710
this is a brand new terminal window,

01:34.710 --> 01:35.730
I just made a new tab.

01:35.730 --> 01:39.120
I haven't moved, I haven't talked about moving.

01:39.120 --> 01:40.500
Where am I?

01:40.500 --> 01:44.310
Well, I'm at a location called the Home Directory

01:44.310 --> 01:45.330
and it's abbreviated

01:45.330 --> 01:48.360
or it's symbolized by this tilde character,

01:48.360 --> 01:51.450
you can find that above a tab key on most keyboards.

01:51.450 --> 01:55.200
Anyway, my home directory contains a lot of things.

01:55.200 --> 01:59.190
If I hit ls, you won't see all of these.

01:59.190 --> 02:00.990
Some of them are standard on a Mac,

02:00.990 --> 02:04.320
like Applications, Desktop, Documents, Downloads,

02:04.320 --> 02:08.280
Public, Pictures, some of them are my folders I've made

02:08.280 --> 02:11.583
like GitDemo and GitStuff.

02:12.450 --> 02:13.283
What else?

02:13.283 --> 02:16.380
Pets, TerminalDemo.

02:16.380 --> 02:18.510
Those are my folders I've made.

02:18.510 --> 02:20.220
Anyway, you'll see something, right?

02:20.220 --> 02:23.040
You'll see contents of your home directory.

02:23.040 --> 02:24.450
And if you're on a Windows machine

02:24.450 --> 02:27.840
we can do the same thing using Git Bash so ls.

02:27.840 --> 02:29.250
Now, I see different things

02:29.250 --> 02:31.470
because there are different files and folders

02:31.470 --> 02:34.650
on a Windows machine but I'm in the same location

02:34.650 --> 02:39.150
on this Windows machine, tilde, that is my home directory.

02:39.150 --> 02:41.460
Alright, so we can't move right now,

02:41.460 --> 02:44.940
we're just simply looking at files and folders

02:44.940 --> 02:47.370
in one location and I'm gonna show you a command

02:47.370 --> 02:49.380
that I don't have a slide for,

02:49.380 --> 02:51.390
on a Mac the command is open.

02:51.390 --> 02:56.390
If we type open. it's actually going to open up in Finder,

02:56.610 --> 02:59.490
on a Mac the File Explorer is called Finder,

02:59.490 --> 03:01.770
the same directory where I currently am.

03:01.770 --> 03:03.272
So this is my home directory

03:03.272 --> 03:05.310
and if you've never used a terminal

03:05.310 --> 03:06.330
you're probably familiar

03:06.330 --> 03:08.343
with this sort of navigation, right?

03:08.343 --> 03:12.420
If I'm wanna look at, my courses folder, I double click.

03:12.420 --> 03:15.003
If I wanna go back, I click the back arrow.

03:16.200 --> 03:19.650
That's kind of the basic navigation without the terminal.

03:19.650 --> 03:21.150
Now if I'm on a Windows machine,

03:21.150 --> 03:23.253
the Open Command actually does not work.

03:24.690 --> 03:27.300
It tells me, "Command not found."

03:27.300 --> 03:30.150
But instead I can do Start.

03:30.150 --> 03:31.710
this is really the one major difference

03:31.710 --> 03:33.330
I can think of so far.

03:33.330 --> 03:36.210
And then this opens up the Windows equivalent of Finder

03:36.210 --> 03:37.830
I think it's called file...

03:37.830 --> 03:39.210
Is it File Explorer?

03:39.210 --> 03:41.220
I'm not much of a Windows user.

03:41.220 --> 03:43.380
Anyway, probably shouldn't admit that.

03:43.380 --> 03:45.990
You can see the folders and files that are listed here

03:45.990 --> 03:48.210
so we've got pictures as an example.

03:48.210 --> 03:50.280
And if I do start. I can see,

03:50.280 --> 03:51.960
all right here's the pictures folder.

03:51.960 --> 03:55.500
We have videos, there's a videos folder there.

03:55.500 --> 03:57.990
So all of those things that we see represented here

03:57.990 --> 04:01.440
are also here, it's just two different ways of interacting

04:01.440 --> 04:04.290
or viewing the contents of this folder.

04:04.290 --> 04:07.110
Alright, so that's ls on its own.

04:07.110 --> 04:09.960
Just ls, nothing afterwards you hit Enter

04:09.960 --> 04:13.050
and it shows us what it's in the current directory

04:13.050 --> 04:16.470
but I can also use ls on a folder.

04:16.470 --> 04:19.860
So I could say, what's inside this Pets folder

04:19.860 --> 04:22.530
and all I need to do is reference the path.

04:22.530 --> 04:26.080
So the name in this case is Pets so ls pets

04:27.030 --> 04:30.085
and that's what's inside that Pets folder.

04:30.085 --> 04:32.250
And I can actually open that folder too

04:32.250 --> 04:33.940
if I just do open Pets

04:35.190 --> 04:38.220
and it opened on my other screen, let me drag this up.

04:38.220 --> 04:40.020
There we are, you can see those contents,

04:40.020 --> 04:42.420
Cats, Chickens, Hamsters, and so on.

04:42.420 --> 04:45.090
Alright, I'll do that on a Windows machine as well.

04:45.090 --> 04:46.080
Let's say I wanna know

04:46.080 --> 04:49.710
what is inside of this GitCourse directory.

04:49.710 --> 04:53.610
So I can do ls git and I need to spell it correctly

04:53.610 --> 04:56.370
but I can also hit Tab to auto complete

04:56.370 --> 04:57.930
and it looks like it's an empty directory.

04:57.930 --> 04:59.970
Well, that's my fault for picking that one.

04:59.970 --> 05:00.960
Let's try another one.

05:00.960 --> 05:04.290
How about what is in GitStuff?

05:04.290 --> 05:07.260
I don't know, I don't remember making that

05:07.260 --> 05:09.243
but we'll try ls GitStuff.

05:10.290 --> 05:12.720
Okay, there's one folder called DEMO.

05:12.720 --> 05:15.375
Not very exciting, but hopefully you get the idea here.

05:15.375 --> 05:18.690
We can use ls with nothing afterwards.

05:18.690 --> 05:20.430
Also, there's a command called the Clear,

05:20.430 --> 05:22.680
I'll just show because I'll be using this

05:22.680 --> 05:23.513
it kind of annoys me

05:23.513 --> 05:26.523
when I have a lot of stuff in my terminal so that goes away.

05:27.390 --> 05:29.417
So that has nothing to do with navigation.

05:29.417 --> 05:31.500
So as I was saying, ls on its own

05:31.500 --> 05:33.900
will list the contents of our current directory,

05:33.900 --> 05:36.930
ls followed by the name of a directory...

05:36.930 --> 05:39.630
like I could do Pets, as I've shown you,

05:39.630 --> 05:43.470
I see the files in Pets, it's a bunch of folders actually.

05:43.470 --> 05:47.610
And if I wanted to look at, let's say the Goats directory

05:47.610 --> 05:51.510
or how about the Cats directory, I can't do ls Cats

05:51.510 --> 05:54.750
because there is no Cats directory where I currently am.

05:54.750 --> 05:56.850
Remember this is what I see, right?

05:56.850 --> 06:00.510
I currently am viewing or I'm inside this home directory,

06:00.510 --> 06:02.212
that's what I see when I type ls.

06:02.212 --> 06:06.060
I have to type out the path to access Cats.

06:06.060 --> 06:11.060
It's inside of Pets so it's actually this ls pets/Cats

06:12.180 --> 06:13.013
and there we are.

06:13.013 --> 06:15.833
It's one of my cats, her name is Blue, Blue Steele.

06:15.833 --> 06:18.690
And that's all I'll show you with ls,

06:18.690 --> 06:22.170
we can reference these paths where we chain things together.

06:22.170 --> 06:25.710
Anyway, the next command I'll show you is pretty simple.

06:25.710 --> 06:29.880
It's called pwd for print working directory.

06:29.880 --> 06:32.430
And what this will do, it's very simple

06:32.430 --> 06:34.680
it just prints out your current location.

06:34.680 --> 06:38.130
It prints out the path to where you currently are.

06:38.130 --> 06:39.870
So we haven't been able to move, right?

06:39.870 --> 06:42.510
We haven't changed locations yet.

06:42.510 --> 06:46.290
We've been able to like peek into other folders with ls

06:46.290 --> 06:50.460
but pwd is, if I just type it like that,

06:50.460 --> 06:53.790
it's gonna give me the exact location where I am right now.

06:53.790 --> 06:56.340
So the home directory here is just shorts...

06:56.340 --> 06:58.320
That little tilde is a short way

06:58.320 --> 07:00.510
of referencing this location.

07:00.510 --> 07:02.850
My user on here is called coltsteele.

07:02.850 --> 07:04.440
That is where I currently am.

07:04.440 --> 07:06.870
But if I'm on my Windows machine, it's gonna...

07:06.870 --> 07:08.370
It's quite different.

07:08.370 --> 07:10.050
It's similar but it's different

07:10.050 --> 07:12.780
because on a Windows machine we've got this C Drive, right?

07:12.780 --> 07:15.990
So c/Users/coltsteele

07:15.990 --> 07:18.960
and that's obviously going to be your username,

07:18.960 --> 07:20.940
so probably not coltsteele.

07:20.940 --> 07:23.190
So that is where we currently are,

07:23.190 --> 07:25.950
if I type pwd, that's my location.

07:25.950 --> 07:27.630
And that brings me to our next command,

07:27.630 --> 07:30.870
which is CD for change directory,

07:30.870 --> 07:32.790
this is how we move around.

07:32.790 --> 07:35.430
It's the equivalent if I do open,

07:35.430 --> 07:37.380
it's the equivalent of double clicking

07:37.380 --> 07:38.730
on one of these things, right?

07:38.730 --> 07:40.200
And moving between folders.

07:40.200 --> 07:42.090
I'm now in a different folder,

07:42.090 --> 07:44.550
you could think of, this is my current directory.

07:44.550 --> 07:47.400
Now if I click here, this is my current directory.

07:47.400 --> 07:50.430
And if I click in Pictures, that's my current directory

07:50.430 --> 07:52.590
and there's one of my chicken's, Stevie Chicks.

07:52.590 --> 07:53.460
All right?

07:53.460 --> 07:56.550
So if I want to change directories from the terminal,

07:56.550 --> 07:58.650
which I'll be doing all the time in the course,

07:58.650 --> 08:01.680
I use CD followed by the directory

08:01.680 --> 08:03.243
that I'm trying to change into.

08:05.100 --> 08:07.380
So I often use ls first.

08:07.380 --> 08:08.460
What do we have?

08:08.460 --> 08:10.860
Let's go into this code directory.

08:10.860 --> 08:11.983
You probably don't have that

08:11.983 --> 08:16.170
but you could pick any other folder of yours and CD into it.

08:16.170 --> 08:19.290
So cs Code
(Instructor typing)

08:19.290 --> 08:21.570
all right, so my prompt changes

08:21.570 --> 08:23.760
it tells me I'm in the Code folder.

08:23.760 --> 08:25.680
Your prompt will look different but it should...

08:25.680 --> 08:28.530
It likely will reveal to you where you are

08:28.530 --> 08:31.290
but if not, you can always type pwd.

08:31.290 --> 08:35.940
And we see, "Oh, now I'm in Users/coltsteele/Code.

08:35.940 --> 08:37.380
So just to illustrate this,

08:37.380 --> 08:39.870
if I were to open the current folder

08:39.870 --> 08:43.740
now I'm in the Code folder, not the home directory.

08:43.740 --> 08:47.873
And I can continue to move but first, if I type ls

08:47.873 --> 08:50.460
now we see completely different contents

08:50.460 --> 08:52.950
a lot of stuff in this Code folder,

08:52.950 --> 08:55.020
this is from one of my other courses

08:55.020 --> 08:58.350
and I could take a look at any of these other locations.

08:58.350 --> 08:59.730
How about, hmm...

08:59.730 --> 09:03.000
CSS Intro, O4 CSS_Intro

09:03.000 --> 09:07.383
So cd 04 and I'm just gonna hit tab to auto complete for me.

09:08.340 --> 09:13.340
And if I CD into that, I type pwd, if I can spell it.

09:13.620 --> 09:15.360
Notice my working directory,

09:15.360 --> 09:17.670
my current location has changed.

09:17.670 --> 09:20.340
And if I type ls, we now see the contents.

09:20.340 --> 09:24.360
And if I open, this is what we're looking at in finder.

09:24.360 --> 09:29.360
text_properties, css_intro, and then a zip file right there.

09:29.880 --> 09:31.560
Same thing on a Windows machine.

09:31.560 --> 09:36.480
If I pick some folder to CD into, how about...

09:36.480 --> 09:38.160
Well, I don't really know, I don't have much on here

09:38.160 --> 09:41.430
but let's go into desktop maybe,

09:41.430 --> 09:42.630
I'm not sure if it's on there.

09:42.630 --> 09:46.590
So cs desktop
(Instructor typing)

09:46.590 --> 09:50.520
and I'm gonna clear and now if I type pwd,

09:50.520 --> 09:53.550
my working directory, my location has changed.

09:53.550 --> 09:57.750
I'm now on my home directory c/user/coltsteele

09:57.750 --> 10:00.690
on a Windows machine /desktop.

10:00.690 --> 10:03.540
If I type ls oh, there's nothing on here.

10:03.540 --> 10:05.670
Well, that's underwhelming.
(Instructor laughs)

10:05.670 --> 10:08.730
And that actually leads me to my next command,

10:08.730 --> 10:11.310
which is a variation of cd,

10:11.310 --> 10:15.390
cd followed by two dots, this is how we move backwards.

10:15.390 --> 10:17.880
So remember I showed you this example in Finder

10:17.880 --> 10:19.380
if I open this up.

10:19.380 --> 10:21.990
if I want to CD into this...

10:21.990 --> 10:24.930
Cd, if I want to double click in and then I decide,

10:24.930 --> 10:27.630
ah I want to go back, I just click the back arrow.

10:27.630 --> 10:31.617
But using cd, what we actually can do is cd..

10:32.730 --> 10:36.750
and this will take us back one level, one folder.

10:36.750 --> 10:38.070
So we back up.

10:38.070 --> 10:42.360
So currently I'm in this CSS_Intro folder,

10:42.360 --> 10:43.980
this is what's inside of it.

10:43.980 --> 10:48.980
If I do cd.. and I type pwd this is where I am now.

10:49.860 --> 10:51.390
That's where I was before,

10:51.390 --> 10:54.510
I'm now one level back, right here.

10:54.510 --> 10:57.090
And I could do it again cd.. first,

10:57.090 --> 11:01.437
let me just show you the contents all that stuff, cd..

11:02.880 --> 11:05.250
now I'm back in my home directory.

11:05.250 --> 11:06.660
So same thing over here,

11:06.660 --> 11:08.970
I'm in the desktop or I'm on the desktop,

11:08.970 --> 11:11.400
there's nothing to look at, it's empty.

11:11.400 --> 11:15.780
cd.. and now I'm in my home directory.

11:15.780 --> 11:18.180
So that's the very basics of navigation.

11:18.180 --> 11:19.950
We'll get more practice in the next video

11:19.950 --> 11:22.500
as we talk more about some other commands,

11:22.500 --> 11:23.970
like making folders and files,

11:23.970 --> 11:25.920
and deleting folders and files.

11:25.920 --> 11:27.150
Along with those commands.

11:27.150 --> 11:31.590
We still need to use cd, pwd, and ls all the time.

11:31.590 --> 11:33.360
So you'll get practice if you're new to this

11:33.360 --> 11:37.050
but also keep in mind, you're not gonna just memorize it

11:37.050 --> 11:39.930
